2️⃣ Theory
What is a Set?

A Set is an unordered, mutable collection that stores only unique values.

Characteristics:

Stores unique elements
Removes duplicates automatically
Unordered
Mutable
Fast membership checking
Uses Hash Tables internally

Example:

countries = {
    "Thailand",
    "Japan",
    "Dubai"
}
Creating an Empty Set

Correct:

countries = set()

Incorrect:

countries = {}

{} creates an empty Dictionary, not a Set.

Adding Elements
countries.add("Singapore")
Removing Elements

Using remove():

countries.remove("Japan")

Raises an error if the element doesn't exist.

Using discard():

countries.discard("Japan")

No error if the element doesn't exist.

Membership Checking
if "Thailand" in countries:
    print("Available")

Very fast because Sets use Hash Tables.

Average Time Complexity:

O(1)

Looping Through a Set
for country in countries:
    print(country)

The order is not guaranteed.

Set Operations
Union

Returns all unique elements.

asia | europe
Intersection

Returns common elements.

visited & wishlist
Difference

Returns elements present in the first Set but not the second.

visited - wishlist
Symmetric Difference

Returns elements present in either Set but not both.

visited ^ wishlist
☕ 3️⃣ Java Comparison

Python Set is equivalent to Java's:

HashSet<String> countries = new HashSet<>();

Java:

countries.add("Thailand");
countries.add("Japan");
countries.add("Thailand");

Only one "Thailand" is stored.

Python:

countries = {
    "Thailand",
    "Japan",
    "Thailand"
}

Same behavior.

⭐ 4️⃣ Important Concepts
Hash Table

Python Sets use Hash Tables internally.

Benefits:

Fast searching
Fast insertion
Fast deletion

Average Complexity:

Operation	Complexity
Add	O(1)
Remove	O(1)
Membership (in)	O(1)
Iterate	O(n)
Set Operations
Operator	Meaning
`	`
&	Intersection
-	Difference
^	Symmetric Difference
List vs Set
List	Set
Ordered	Unordered
Allows duplicates	Removes duplicates
Indexing supported	No indexing
Slower membership check	Faster membership check
✅ 5️⃣ Best Practices

✔ Use a Set when duplicate values should not exist.

✔ Use Sets for fast membership testing.

✔ Use discard() when you're unsure if an element exists.

✔ Use meaningful variable names.

Example:

visited_destinations

instead of

s

✔ Store the result of Set operations in variables when they will be reused.

Example:

common_destinations = visited & wishlist

print(common_destinations)
