🎤 8️⃣ Basic Interview Questions & Answers
Q1. What is a Set?

A mutable collection that stores only unique values.

Q2. Does a Set allow duplicates?

No.

Duplicate elements are automatically removed.

Q3. How do you create an empty Set?
set()
Q4. Difference between remove() and discard()?

remove() raises an error if the element doesn't exist.

discard() does not raise an error.

Q5. Can Sets be indexed?

No.

Sets are unordered.

Q6. What is the average time complexity of membership checking?

O(1)

Q7. Which operator performs Union?
|
Q8. Which operator performs Intersection?
&
Q9. Which operator performs Difference?
-
Q10. Which operator performs Symmetric Difference?
^
💼 9️⃣ Senior Engineer Interview Questions & Answers
Q1. Why are Sets faster than Lists?

Sets use Hash Tables, allowing average O(1) membership checks.

Lists require linear searching, resulting in O(n) complexity.

Q2. When should you use a Set instead of a List?

When:

Removing duplicates
Fast searching
Comparing collections
Unique values are required
Q3. Can a Set contain Lists?

No.

Lists are mutable and therefore unhashable.

Q4. Can a Set contain Tuples?

Yes.

Tuples are immutable and hashable.

Example:

locations = {
    ("Delhi", 28.61),
    ("Mumbai", 19.07)
}
Q5. How are Sets implemented internally?

Using Hash Tables.

Q6. What are common production use cases for Sets?
User permissions
Unique email addresses
Tags
Removing duplicate records
Fast lookups
Recommendation systems
Q7. What is the difference between a Dictionary and a Set?

Dictionary:

Stores key-value pairs.

Set:

Stores only unique values.

